var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.WavePacket", Doc: "WavePacket returns value for a gaussian * cosine wave packet for given\nlinear dimension value x and 3D distance d.", Args: []string{"x", "d", "wavelength", "width", "phase", "amp"}, Returns: []string{"float32"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.KleinGordonKernel", Doc: "KleinGordonKernel is the kernel for computing the KleinGordon equations,\non scalar state values (WaveStates).", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.KleinGordonCKernel", Doc: "KleinGordonCKernel is the kernel for the complex Klein-Gordon equation, with\noptional minimal coupling to the electromagnetic field (Params.EM).\n\nUncoupled, the two components evolve as two independent real KG waves: the\ncomplex structure only shows up in the conserved charge, which needs both.\n\nCoupled, the covariant derivative D = d - i(e/hbar)A adds three terms:\n\n\tdd(phi_a) = c^2 (Lap - mhsq) phi_a + (2e/hbar)(A0 d(phi_b) + c A.grad phi_b) + (e^2/hbar^2)(A0^2 - A^2) phi_a\n\tdd(phi_b) = c^2 (Lap - mhsq) phi_b - (2e/hbar)(A0 d(phi_a) + c A.grad phi_a) + (e^2/hbar^2)(A0^2 - A^2) phi_b\n\nThe A0 term acts on the VELOCITY with opposite signs on the two components,\nwhich is a rotation of (d phi_a, d phi_b) at rate 2 e A0 / hbar. An explicit\nintegrator does not rotate, it spirals, and the charge runs away -- which is\nthe instability described in web/content/complex-kg.md. Params.Boris applies\nthe rotation exactly instead. See EWBorisPhase for the same problem in the\nelectroweak kernel.\n\nIt also writes the [Charge] and [Current] that MaxwellKernel reads, so the\nwave and the field drive each other.", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.KleinGordonDampKernel", Doc: "KleinGordonDampKernel is the kernel for computing the KleinGordon equations,\non scalar state values (WaveStates),\nat damped edges. Does Sommerfield damping where velocity = force.", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.KleinGordonCDampKernel", Doc: "KleinGordonCDampKernel is the kernel for computing the KleinGordonC equations,\non complex wave state,\nat damped edges. Does Sommerfield damping where velocity = force.", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargeAtRest", Doc: "ChargeAtRest is a gaussian lump of charge sitting still: a particle, as far\nas this equation has one.\n\nThe two components a quarter cycle apart are a complex number turning at the\nrest mass frequency Omega0 = m c^2 / hbar, and that turning IS the charge:\n\n\trho = (hbar e / m c^2) (phi_b d phi_a - phi_a d phi_b) = -e |chi|^2\n\nexactly, at every cell. Watch the total charge stat while the blob spreads:\na lump at rest is a spread of momenta, so it disperses as any massive wave\npacket must, and the charge is conserved through all of it while |chi|^2\nmoves around. No single real field has a quantity like that, which is the\nreason for going complex in the first place.\n\nReverse the sense of rotation and the charge changes sign, with nothing else\nabout the wave any different. That is antimatter, in the only sense this\nequation knows about it.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargeAtRestAnti", Doc: "ChargeAtRestAnti is [ChargeAtRest] turning the other way: the same wave with\nthe opposite charge.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargeUniform", Doc: "ChargeUniform fills all of space with charge at rest: flat and dull to look\nat, but rho is then exactly constant in space and time, which makes it the\nclean case for checking the charge against its analytic value.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.kgSelfScale", Doc: "kgSelfScale sets Mu0 so the potential a charge blob makes for itself is the\ngiven fraction of its own rest energy.\n\nA gaussian charge density of peak rho0 = e amp^2 and width w sits at the\ncentre of its own potential\n\n\tA0(0) = Mu0 rho0 w^2 / 2\n\nand the coupling only means anything while e A0 is well under m c^2. Past\nthat the potential exceeds the rest energy, which is the Klein regime: the\nsingle-particle equation stops describing one particle and the numbers stop\nmaking sense soon after.\n\nAt the defaults that bound is tight. m c^2 = 0.031 while Mu0 = e = 1 and a\nblob of amp 0.05 and width 12 reaches e A0 = m c^2 within five steps. There\nis no amplitude that is both visible and stable, because rho goes as amp^2\nwhile the display range does not -- Mu0 is the knob, not the amplitude.\nReducing it is not a fudge: the real coupling is weak too, which is what the\nfine structure constant being 1/137 says.", Args: []string{"ss", "frac"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargeSelfField", Doc: "ChargeSelfField is [ChargeAtRest] with the back-reaction on: the charge\nmakes its own electromagnetic potential, and is pushed by it.\n\nMu0 is set from [kgSelfScale] so the self-potential is a twentieth of the\nrest energy, which is the regime where this is a charge with a field rather\nthan a numerical accident. Raise the fraction and it goes sooner.\n\nEdges are damped, not wrapped. A net charge in a periodic box has no\nsolution to Poisson's equation at all -- every copy of the box adds another\ncharge -- so A0 climbs without bound and takes the run with it eventually,\nhowever small the amplitude.\n\nThe blob is not bound and is not supposed to be: like charge repels itself,\nso it spreads faster than free dispersion alone and eventually leaves\nthrough the walls, taking its charge with it. There is nothing in this\nequation that could hold a charge together, which is a real feature of it\nrather than a limitation of the simulation.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargedPacket", Doc: "ChargedPacket is a moving charge: a wave packet in the two components a\nquarter wavelength apart, travelling along X.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.scalarAtom", Doc: "KGCConfigs are the initialization options offered in the GUI.\nscalarAtom is the shared setup for the two bound-state configs: an external\nCoulomb well, no self-field, damped edges so whatever is not held can leave.", Args: []string{"ss", "a", "n"}, Returns: []string{"float32"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ScalarHydrogen", Doc: "ScalarHydrogen is the same atom as HydrogenGround, but from the relativistic\nwave equation: a spin-0 particle bound in a Coulomb well, which is what a\npionic atom actually is.\n\nThe shape is the nonrelativistic exp(-r/a), which is an eigenstate only to\norder (Z alpha)^2 -- the true Klein-Gordon Coulomb state carries an extra\nr^s with s = -(Z alpha)^2 near the origin. At Z alpha = 0.2 that is a few\npercent, so the state stays put but breathes slightly rather than sitting\nperfectly still. The breathing IS the relativistic correction.\n\nThe uniform phase rate is what makes it a bound state at all: one frequency\neverywhere, E = m c^2 + binding. Compare Charge At Rest, which uses the local\nrate and is not an eigenstate of anything.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ScalarHydrogenP", Doc: "ScalarHydrogenP is the 2p orbital of the same equation: two lobes of\nopposite phase with a node between them, held by a well four times deeper so\nthat it comes out the size of the ground state above.\n\nA spin-0 particle can sit in an l = 1 orbital and this is what it looks like.\nThe deeper well puts it at Z alpha = 0.4, which for l = 0 would be close to\nthe Z alpha = 1/2 where the Klein-Gordon Coulomb ground state stops existing\nat all; l = 1 is safe until 3/2, so the p state tolerates a far heavier\nnucleus than the s state does.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.MaxwellKernel", Doc: "MaxwellKernel is the kernel for computing the Maxwell EM equations,\non EM state values (EMStates).", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.MaxwellDampKernel", Doc: "MaxwellDampKernel is the kernel for computing the Maxwell EM equations,\non EM state values (EMStates),\nat damped edges. Does Sommerfield damping where velocity = force.", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.MaxwellViewAll", Doc: "MaxwellViewAll configures the View to display all Maxwell values", Args: []string{"view"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.emSourceI", Doc: "emSourceI returns the integer cell of [Config.Source], where negatives mean\nthe center.", Args: []string{"ss"}, Returns: []string{"Vector3i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.emTransverse", Doc: "emTransverse returns the potential and velocity variables for the transverse\ndirection [Config.Polarization], and the orthogonal pair. A wave running\nalong X can be polarized along Y or Z, and nothing else.", Args: []string{"ss"}, Returns: []string{"pos", "vel", "orthoPos", "orthoVel"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ElectricPotential", Doc: "ElectricPotential puts a point charge at [Config.Source] and lets the scalar\npotential build up around it: A0 falls off as 1/r, and E = -grad A0 as 1/r^2.\n\nWith Params.A0NoWave on (the default) the A0 update sets velocity from force\nrather than accumulating it, which makes it Jacobi relaxation of Poisson's\nequation instead of a wave. The potential fills in from the charge outward\nand settles. It is not quick: relaxation is diffusive, so reaching the far\nwall takes on the order of (L/pi)^2 / C^2 steps.\n\nEdges are FIXED, which holds A0 at zero on the walls: a charge inside a\ngrounded metal box. Wrapping would put an image charge in every neighboring\ncopy and never settle, and damping leaves the wall floating, so A0 comes out\nas 1/r plus an arbitrary constant. Grounding fixes that constant, at the\nprice of a known one: the solution is\n\n\tA0 = k (1/r - 1/R)\n\nwith R the distance to the wall, which is the Dirichlet Green's function.\nNear the charge it is 1/r; near the wall it is bent down to meet zero.\n\nWatch the A0 radial stats: the X and diagonal samples at the same radius\nshould agree, which is what the 19-point stencil buys.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.ChargeDipole", Doc: "ChargeDipole is two opposite charges, one [Config.Wavelength] either side of\n[Config.Source] along X.\n\nThe far field is the dipole one, falling as 1/r^3 rather than 1/r^2, because\nthe two potentials cancel to leading order. Along the axis between them E is\nstrongest and points from positive to negative; on the perpendicular bisector\nit is half the strength and points the other way.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.PolarizedPhoton", Doc: "PolarizedPhoton sends a linearly polarized electromagnetic packet along X.\n\nOnly the vector potential is set, in the single transverse component\n[Config.Polarization]. Everything else follows from the kernel: E = -dA/dt\nis along that same component, and B = curl A is along the OTHER transverse\none. So with Y polarization you get E along Y and B along Z, and with Z\npolarization E along Z and B along -Y. E, B and the direction of travel are\nmutually perpendicular, and there is nothing along X: that is what makes it\na transverse wave, and it is the whole content of \"polarization\" for a plane\nwave -- which of the two transverse directions E points along.\n\nA longitudinal wave is not available to pick, and that is not an omission:\nsetting AX instead would be pure gauge, giving E = B = 0.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.CircularPolarization", Doc: "CircularPolarization sends a circularly polarized packet along X: both\ntransverse components at once, a quarter cycle out of step.\n\nA_pol goes as cos(kx - wt) and the orthogonal component as sin(kx - wt), so\ntheir sum has constant magnitude and a direction that turns. At a fixed\npoint E rotates in the transverse plane once per period; at a fixed time it\ntraces a helix along X. Neither component is doing anything a linear wave\ndoes not do -- circular polarization is not a third kind of wave, it is two\nlinear ones added with a phase offset, which is why any polarization can be\nwritten in either basis.\n\n[Config.Polarization] picks which component leads, and that is the\nhandedness: Y leading Z turns one way, Z leading Y the other.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.StandingWave", Doc: "StandingWave fills the box with a sinusoidal vector potential and NO\nvelocity, which is equal parts running each way: a standing wave.\n\nA = cos(kx) held still evolves as cos(kx) cos(wt), so E = -dA/dt goes as\ncos(kx) sin(wt) and B = curl A as sin(kx) cos(wt). Their nodes are a quarter\nwavelength apart -- E vanishes where B peaks -- and they are also a quarter\nPERIOD apart in time. That double offset is why a standing wave stores\nenergy without transporting it: E x B averages to zero both ways.\n\nEdges wrap, so put a whole number of wavelengths in the box or the two ends\nwill not meet.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.MovingCharge", Doc: "MovingCharge shows the magnetic field that moving charge produces: a\nuniformly charged line along X, moving along its own length at\n[Config.Velocity].X, which is a current.\n\nA line rather than a blob, because this kernel has no advection: charge and\ncurrent are set once and stay put. A line of charge sliding along itself is\nthe one moving-charge configuration for which that is exactly right, since\nthe density it leaves behind is the density it had. Anything localized would\nneed the charge to actually move, and would violate continuity.\n\nThe result is E pointing radially away from the line and B circling around\nit, with |B|/|E| = v/c^2. Set Config.Velocity.X to zero and B disappears\nwhile E stays: magnetism is what electric charge looks like when it moves.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.SetParticleAt", Doc: "SetParticleAt sets particle dynamic data at given index.", Args: []string{"idx", "lorentz", "esq", "pos", "vel"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.GetParticleAt", Doc: "GetParticleAt gets particle dynamic data at given index.", Args: []string{"idx"}, Returns: []string{"lorentz", "esq", "pos", "vel"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.NewPlaneMesh", Doc: "NewPlaneMesh adds PlaneMesh mesh to given scene for given layer", Args: []string{"sc", "view", "panel"}, Returns: []string{"PlaneMesh"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.SchrodingerKernel", Doc: "SchrodingerKernel is the kernel for Schrodinger's equation, as a staggered\nleapfrog on the real and imaginary parts.\n\nSplitting i hbar d(chi)/dt = H chi into chi = a + ib gives\n\n\tda/dt = -(hbar/2m) Lap b + (V/hbar) b\n\tdb/dt = +(hbar/2m) Lap a - (V/hbar) a\n\nso each part is driven entirely by the other. Updating both from the same\nold values is unstable -- it is a rotation being integrated by a method that\ndoes not rotate, the same failure the complex Klein-Gordon equation has\nwithout Params.Boris. The fix is to stagger them: advance a using the old b,\nthen advance b using the NEW a. That is Visscher's scheme -- see Visscher\n1991, building on Askar & Cakmak 1978 -- which is explicitly stable, second\norder, and conserves a modified norm exactly.\n\nIt is also the only form this can take in a cellular automaton. Advancing b\nwith the new a means reading the new a at the NEIGHBORS, which one local\npass cannot see -- so the update has to alternate between steps, a on even\nand b on odd, with the other carried forward. The alternation is not a hack\naround the instability; it is what makes the stable scheme local. Two sim\nsteps make one full leapfrog step, so time runs at half rate here compared\nwith the second-order equations: Sim.TimePerStep is 0.5, which the stats use.\n\nNote there is no C here at all. This is the nonrelativistic limit, and the\nonly rate in it is hbar / 2m; a wave moves at hbar k / m, not at some\nfraction of a light speed the equation never mentions.\n\nStability needs (hbar / 2m) < about 1: the Laplacian's extreme eigenvalue is\nnear 2 and the leapfrog tolerates a product under 2. SchrodingerConfig sets\na mass that satisfies it, which the Klein-Gordon default does NOT.", Directives: []types.Directive{{Tool: "gosl", Directive: "start"}, {Tool: "gosl", Directive: "kernel"}}, Args: []string{"i"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.schrodOmega", Doc: "schrodOmega returns the angular frequency of the harmonic well, in radians\nper LEAPFROG step. A sim step advances half of one, so the period you count\non screen is twice 2 pi / omega.", Args: []string{"ss"}, Returns: []string{"float32"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.HarmonicOscillator", Doc: "HarmonicOscillator is the classic: a particle in a parabolic well\nV = (1/2) m omega^2 r^2, started as its own ground state pushed off center.\n\nThat displaced ground state is a coherent state, and it is the one quantum\nstate that behaves exactly like a classical particle: it swings back and\nforth at omega and does NOT spread, because the well squeezes it back\ntogether as fast as dispersion pulls it apart. Any other starting width\nbreathes as well as swinging.\n\nThe ground state width is sqrt(hbar / m omega), which is set from the period\nrather than chosen, so the two are not free to disagree.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.BoxStandingWave", Doc: "BoxStandingWave is a particle in a box: the walls hold the wave at zero, and\nthe state is the lowest standing wave that fits between them.\n\nIt is an energy eigenstate, so nothing about |chi|^2 moves at all -- the two\ncomponents turn into each other at the rate E / hbar and their squares add\nto a constant. Stationary states being genuinely stationary is the whole\ncontent of \"energy level\", and it is worth seeing that it is not an\napproximation here.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.BoxTwoStates", Doc: "BoxTwoStates is the same box holding the lowest two standing waves at once.\n\nNeither one moves on its own, but they turn at different rates, E1/hbar and\nE2/hbar, so their sum beats: |chi|^2 sloshes from one side to the other and\nback at the DIFFERENCE frequency. Every visible motion in a bound quantum\nsystem is a beat between levels like this one.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.HydrogenGround", Doc: "HydrogenGround is as close to a hydrogen atom as this equation gets: an\nattractive 1/r well with the electron in its ground state, exp(-r/a).\n\nFor V = -k/r the ground state is exp(-r/a) with a = hbar^2 / m k, so the\nwell depth is set from the Bohr radius here rather than picked, which is\nwhat makes the state actually stationary. Watch Mag sit still.\n\nTwo caveats worth knowing. The 1/r singularity is softened to its value one\ncube out, since a lattice cannot hold an infinity, and exp(-r/a) has its\ncusp exactly there -- so the peak is not the lattice eigenstate and relaxes\nat once, after which |chi|^2 at the center swings over about 40% of its\nstarting value. The shape away from the core barely moves; it is the cusp\nthat is wrong. And this is one electron in a fixed external potential, not a\nnucleus and an electron finding each other -- there is no back-reaction, and\nno second electron, so no chemistry.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.HydrogenP", Doc: "HydrogenP is the 2p orbital: two lobes of opposite phase, one above and one\nbelow a node through the center, which is the l = 1 state everyone draws.\n\nThe well here is four times deeper than HydrogenGround's, so that the n = 2\norbital, which decays over 2a rather than a, comes out the same size and\nfits the same box. The sign flip between the lobes is invisible in |chi|^2,\nand is what stops them from merging into one lump.\n\nThe node is exact and stays exact: it is fixed by symmetry, not by getting\nthe radial function right, so it holds to a fraction of a percent while the\nlobes breathe by about as much as the ground state does, and for the same\nlattice reason.\n\nThe node is why a p electron feels the nucleus less than an s one, and the\nlobes are why bonds built from them point in particular directions.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

var _ = types.AddFunc(&types.Func{Name: "github.com/WaveReality/waves/wavesim.FreePacket", Doc: "FreePacket is a gaussian wave packet moving along X with nothing to push it:\nit travels at the group velocity and spreads, which is the one thing a free\nquantum particle reliably does.", Args: []string{"ss"}})

